#
# For a description of the syntax of this configuration file,
# see the file kconfig-language.txt in the NuttX tools repository.
#

config INPUT_GT911
    bool "Goodix GT911 touch controller"
    default n
    depends on I2C
    select INPUT_TOUCHSCREEN
    ---help---
        Enable the Goodix GT911 (and GT9-family compatible) capacitive touch
        controller driver.

        The driver is a lower half of the generic NuttX touchscreen framework
        (CONFIG_INPUT_TOUCHSCREEN, /dev/inputN), so it can be consumed by
        LVGL through CONFIG_LV_USE_NUTTX_TOUCHSCREEN or read directly.  It
        reports every contact in the controller's frame (up to
        INPUT_GT911_MAX_POINTS) with the track id the controller assigns.

        Nothing that belongs to a specific module is compiled into the driver:
        the reset and interrupt lines come in through struct gt911_board_s,
        the configuration table for a module that arrived without one comes in
        through struct gt911_config_s, and the coordinate orientation -- how
        the module is mounted relative to the framebuffer, which is a property
        of the product rather than of the chip -- is passed to
        gt911_register().  That keeps one driver usable by several boards
        whose panels are mounted differently, and lets a board derive the
        touch orientation and the display orientation from one setting so the
        two cannot disagree.

        See boards/rk3576/drivers/include/gt911.h for all three.

if INPUT_GT911

config INPUT_GT911_I2C_FREQUENCY
    int "I2C frequency (Hz)"
    default 400000
    range 0 400000
    ---help---
        I2C bus frequency used to talk to the controller.  400 kHz is the
        controller's maximum and needs adequate external pull-ups on SCL/SDA;
        drop to 100000 if the bus wiring is long or the pull-ups are weak.

config INPUT_GT911_MAX_POINTS
    int "Number of touch points reported per sample"
    default 5
    range 1 10
    ---help---
        Number of contact slots in every sample the driver publishes.  This
        value is what the framework reports through TSIOC_GETMAXPOINTS and it
        sizes every sample, so LVGL reads exactly this many points per
        read().

        The GT911 supports up to 5 contacts, but the module's configuration
        may cap it lower; slots with no contact are reported with no flags.
        LVGL uses only the first point unless gesture recognition is enabled.
        Slots beyond the controller's own maximum are simply never used.

config INPUT_GT911_SAMPLE_CACHES
    int "Number of samples buffered by the framework"
    default 4
    range 1 16
    ---help---
        Depth of the touchscreen framework's sample queue.  A deeper queue
        keeps fast bursts of samples from overwriting each other when the
        reader (LVGL) is slower than the controller's scan rate.

config INPUT_GT911_POLL_MS
    int "Fallback polling interval (ms)"
    default 20
    range 0 1000
    ---help---
        The controller raises INT once per scan period while a read frame is
        waiting; with the INT line wired, the interrupt path does the work and
        this poll is only a safety net that repairs a missed edge.

        Set it to 0 for pure interrupt-driven operation (a board without a
        poll still needs the INT line), or to a few tens of milliseconds to
        run interrupt-free.  Interrupts, when wired, always take priority over
        this interval.

config INPUT_GT911_CONFIG_FORCE
    bool "Overwrite the module configuration even if it is already valid"
    default n
    ---help---
        The driver only writes the board's configuration table when the
        controller reports no usable configuration: a module that already
        carries one -- programmed at the factory, or by the vendor's own
        driver -- is left alone, because sensing parameters are a property of
        the touch sensor and a wrong guess senses poorly or not at all.

        Enable this to force a rewrite, which is what trying a different
        table on a module that has already been programmed needs.  Note that
        the controller keeps one configuration: a forced write replaces
        whatever is in there.

        This is a bring-up option, not something a product should ship with
        unless its own table is known to be correct.

endif # INPUT_GT911
